body:
@barbeau Well, here in Europe it is not that rare for a train to change its direction (even multiple times) and still hold its designation, i.e. being modeled as a single trip. At least this is how I handle it. It'd be great if @RachM could check how it is modeled for trains running through e.g. Leipzig Hbf (DB on route Berlin-Erfurt), Hamburg Hbf (direct ICE trains on Berlin-Copenhagen route), Munich Hbf (OEBB on route Salzburg-Ulm) or Kristianstad (Öresundståg on Copenhagen-Karlskrona route). All major stations in Budapest are dead-ends as well, so long distance trains have to change direction there too.

I'm afraid employing direction_id for that purpose doesn't feel right to me semantically. If we would take this way I'd suggest a separate field for it, something like `trip/consist_orientation=FORWARD|BACKWARD`.

But I'm afraid there is another drawback with this approach as there could be only a single `TripDescriptor` (thus single `CarriageDescriptor[]`) at a time. So up till the time of arrival to the dead-end station the `CarriageDescriptor[]` would be reversed for the rest of the journey after such station. I think it'd be better to use approach similar to headsigns - i.e. `trip_headsign` can be overridden with `stop_headsing` later on. Such approach would enable us to describe even adding and removing carriages along the route of the train as well (again a technique still used for long distance trains in Europe). I know it complicates things, but I'm afraid that's reality :). Thoughts?

To clarify, when I say "Carriage sequence will change (reverse)", I mean producers will send `TripDescriptors` with the elements in `carriage_descriptor` reversed. For example, using this scenario
![trips](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6913473/28562858-4286aa16-7167-11e7-9b82-80db9a48d2cb.jpg)
 as a reference, the `CarriageDescriptors` corresponding to a vehicle on a trip with `direction_id = 1` would be:
```
carriage_descriptor {
  label = 'A'
  carriage_sequence: 1
},
carriage_descriptor {
  label = 'B'
  carriage_sequence: 2
},
carriage_descriptor {
  label = 'C'
  carriage_sequence: 3
}
```
While `direction_id = 0` would be:
```
carriage_descriptor {
  label = 'C'
  carriage_sequence: 1
},
carriage_descriptor {
  label = 'B'
  carriage_sequence: 2
},
carriage_descriptor {
  label = 'A'
  carriage_sequence: 3
}
```
Note also producers can change the carriage_descriptor at any time in the trip. If carriages are added/removed at a stop, then producers simply add/remove carriage_descriptor elements.

You're right - I was thinking mainly in terms of VehiclePositions (and hence only considered the **next** station). However, TripUpdates can specify **many** stations, so we need info about the carriage directions for each of them.

I still think it's important to know what both the arriving and departing sequence is at a particular station.

Revising:
- `CarriageDescriptor` still sits in `VehicleDescriptor`, but only contains info about the carriage facilities (no sequencing info). Some of this could actually be moved to GTFS, in a carriages.txt file.
- TUs: `StopTimeUpdates` contain the carriage sequences
- VPs: the carriage sequence is at the top level, and is for the stop specified in `stop_id`

More concretely:
```
message VehicleDescriptor {
  ....
  repeated CarriageDescriptor carriage_descriptor;
  message CarriageDescriptor {
    optional string id = 1;
    ... wifi, occupancy, bicycles etc
  }
}

message CarriageSequence {
  optional string carriage_descriptor_id = 1;
  optional int sequence = 2;
}

message TripUpdate {
  ...
  message StopTimeUpdate {
    ...
    optional string stop_id = 4;
    ...
    repeated CarriageSequence arrival_carriage_sequence = X;
    repeated CarriageSequence departure_carriage_sequence = X+1;
  }
}

message VehiclePosition {
  ...
  optional string stop_id = 7;
  ...
  repeated CarriageSequence arrival_carriage_sequence = X;
  repeated CarriageSequence departure_carriage_sequence = X+1;
}
```

